1
00:00:01,772 --> 00:00:11,476
[SPEAKER_00]: I was wondering if you could say something about this sutta, if you had anything to say about it.

2
00:00:12,676 --> 00:00:19,379
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about Paticcasamuppāda, just a short verse, Samyutta Nikaya 12.41.

3
00:00:19,559 --> 00:00:25,421
[SPEAKER_00]: It says, and which is the noble method

4
00:00:28,559 --> 00:00:30,219
[SPEAKER_00]: When there is this, there is this.

5
00:00:30,460 --> 00:00:33,300
[SPEAKER_00]: When there isn't this, there isn't this.

6
00:00:33,320 --> 00:00:34,401
[SPEAKER_00]: When this arises, this arises.

7
00:00:34,421 --> 00:00:37,381
[SPEAKER_00]: When this ceases, this ceases.

8
00:00:37,441 --> 00:00:38,402
[SPEAKER_00]: That's that noble method.

9
00:00:58,262 --> 00:01:01,605
[SPEAKER_01]: Noble method is noble understanding, noble discernment.

10
00:01:03,487 --> 00:01:06,550
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as the Buddha said, what method is there?

11
00:01:06,630 --> 00:01:08,693
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the one where he properly attends.

12
00:01:09,754 --> 00:01:12,797
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't do paticca-samupāda.

13
00:01:13,738 --> 00:01:15,700
[SPEAKER_01]: You can either see it or not see it.

14
00:01:16,661 --> 00:01:20,244
[SPEAKER_01]: And usually, paticca-samupāda is translated as dependent origination.

15
00:01:21,840 --> 00:01:23,441
[SPEAKER_01]: I suppose, as a translation, it's fine.

16
00:01:24,161 --> 00:01:31,043
[SPEAKER_01]: But the problem is, it carries on a view of an explanatory nature.

17
00:01:32,324 --> 00:01:34,145
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like cause and effect.

18
00:01:34,465 --> 00:01:36,205
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how people regard paticca upādāna.

19
00:01:36,686 --> 00:01:40,047
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, this is the cause, this is the result, this is the new cause, this is the new result.

20
00:01:40,587 --> 00:01:43,008
[SPEAKER_01]: But it isn't, the way the Buddha described it.

21
00:01:43,708 --> 00:01:48,750
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a principle whereby, while this is present, this is also the result of it.

22
00:01:49,690 --> 00:01:51,091
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's a simultaneous presence.

23
00:01:52,121 --> 00:01:57,246
[SPEAKER_01]: So, with the presence of feeling, there is presence of craving in regard to that present feeling.

24
00:01:58,847 --> 00:02:01,990
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not your craving is the result of your feeling.

25
00:02:03,211 --> 00:02:05,493
[SPEAKER_01]: They're simultaneously present to the same extent.

26
00:02:06,414 --> 00:02:14,661
[SPEAKER_01]: Not knowing how to attend that, not seeing a necessary condition that presently endures within your enduring craving, that's how you crave.

27
00:02:17,048 --> 00:02:22,952
[SPEAKER_01]: So, paticca upādāna, it's not an explanation of what happens to you.

28
00:02:23,172 --> 00:02:27,995
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a direct insight of how to uproot things that happen to you.

29
00:02:28,695 --> 00:02:31,697
[SPEAKER_01]: Liability to craving, greed, aversion, delusion, all these things.

30
00:02:31,737 --> 00:02:33,258
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why it's a noble method.

31
00:02:34,219 --> 00:02:35,880
[SPEAKER_01]: Method of overcoming suffering.

32
00:02:37,731 --> 00:02:38,892
[SPEAKER_01]: But, that's what we said.

33
00:02:39,272 --> 00:02:39,873
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you do it?

34
00:02:39,993 --> 00:02:42,174
[SPEAKER_01]: You... Noble disciple means you need to have a right view.

35
00:02:42,755 --> 00:02:44,376
[SPEAKER_01]: And you do it by properly attending.

36
00:02:44,957 --> 00:02:46,678
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're caught up in craving.

37
00:02:47,098 --> 00:02:49,520
[SPEAKER_01]: You catch yourself full of desire for whatever.

38
00:02:50,681 --> 00:02:53,523
[SPEAKER_01]: Sense experience or desire to get rid of something.

39
00:02:54,964 --> 00:02:57,286
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the necessary basis for that desire?

40
00:02:58,487 --> 00:03:00,969
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you have desire without having craving?

41
00:03:02,050 --> 00:03:04,172
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you have... Oh, I desire such and such things.

42
00:03:05,272 --> 00:03:06,553
[SPEAKER_01]: Without craving those things.

43
00:03:06,613 --> 00:03:07,234
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you have that?

44
00:03:08,224 --> 00:03:08,804
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impossible.

45
00:03:11,025 --> 00:03:21,787
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, any form of desire, doesn't matter the specifics of your desire, requires presently enduring craving, attitude of craving.

46
00:03:21,807 --> 00:03:22,687
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can stop there.

47
00:03:22,787 --> 00:03:25,288
[SPEAKER_01]: Now you can say, okay, so my desire is rooted in my craving.

48
00:03:25,368 --> 00:03:27,689
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not, you know, you're not basically overcoming it.

49
00:03:28,749 --> 00:03:33,250
[SPEAKER_01]: You might have acknowledged it, which is a good first step, but that's... that won't overcome it.

50
00:03:34,049 --> 00:03:35,630
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then the Buddha said you attend further.

51
00:03:36,170 --> 00:03:39,291
[SPEAKER_01]: What is another necessary basis within craving?

52
00:03:41,072 --> 00:03:42,613
[SPEAKER_01]: Necessary basis for craving?

53
00:03:43,673 --> 00:03:43,993
[SPEAKER_01]: Feeling.

54
00:03:44,674 --> 00:03:45,074
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

55
00:03:45,374 --> 00:03:51,837
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you must be feeling either pleasant, must be feeling either unpleasant, or must be feeling either neither pleasant or unpleasant.

56
00:03:53,157 --> 00:03:59,260
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you started with, I want this so much that, you know, I'm about to do unwholesome things just to get it.

57
00:04:00,100 --> 00:04:00,740
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't do it.

58
00:04:00,780 --> 00:04:02,021
[SPEAKER_01]: You practice sense restraint.

59
00:04:03,405 --> 00:04:04,666
[SPEAKER_01]: But the desire is still there.

60
00:04:05,426 --> 00:04:11,491
[SPEAKER_01]: Through that desire, you discern the necessity of presently enduring craving, which is basically the container of that desire.

61
00:04:13,152 --> 00:04:20,397
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, through that presently enduring craving, you discern a necessary basis of presently enduring feeling, which is a container for your craving.

62
00:04:22,158 --> 00:04:26,081
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you start dwelling, attending properly to the nature of that present feeling.

63
00:04:26,461 --> 00:04:27,242
[SPEAKER_01]: Have you created it?

64
00:04:28,803 --> 00:04:28,943
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

65
00:04:29,584 --> 00:04:30,264
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you have said it?

66
00:04:30,324 --> 00:04:30,444
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

67
00:04:30,524 --> 00:04:31,385
[SPEAKER_01]: You're subjected to it.

68
00:04:32,464 --> 00:04:35,086
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not yours, even if you want to make it yours.

69
00:04:36,186 --> 00:04:47,012
[SPEAKER_01]: You're only basically maintaining it as yours, through not seeing it that it isn't yours, and through giving in habitually towards the desire that's already contained within the attitude of craving.

70
00:04:49,074 --> 00:04:54,477
[SPEAKER_01]: So, through giving in to the desire, you maintain ignoring the nature of the feeling, which is that it can't be yours.

71
00:04:54,957 --> 00:04:58,279
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you stop ignoring it, you see that it can't be yours, craving disappears.

72
00:05:00,176 --> 00:05:06,461
[SPEAKER_01]: And disappears by being included within the arisen feeling, not by you choosing to, like, pick it and remove it.

73
00:05:06,882 --> 00:05:15,008
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, when the craving disappears... Are you talking about the right... that's the right view, then?

74
00:05:15,048 --> 00:05:16,590
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, all of this, like...

75
00:05:17,753 --> 00:05:22,757
[SPEAKER_01]: If somebody has the right view already, all of this would be just further development of their right view.

76
00:05:23,738 --> 00:05:31,665
[SPEAKER_01]: But even if you don't have the right view, training yourself how to discern craving within the desire, how to discern feeling within craving,

77
00:05:32,346 --> 00:05:33,687
[SPEAKER_01]: Not before or after.

78
00:05:33,727 --> 00:05:37,170
[SPEAKER_01]: Not as a result, a psychologization or explanation.

79
00:05:37,550 --> 00:05:39,071
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I crave because I've... No.

80
00:05:39,711 --> 00:05:42,794
[SPEAKER_01]: While you crave, you discern present enduring feeling.

81
00:05:42,814 --> 00:05:44,155
[SPEAKER_01]: So, while you crave, you feel.

82
00:05:44,835 --> 00:05:46,316
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's the craving in regard to feeling.

83
00:05:46,857 --> 00:05:50,919
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot have craving, second longer, if the feeling disappears.

84
00:05:51,320 --> 00:05:52,220
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can have feeling.

85
00:05:53,141 --> 00:05:55,122
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you can have feeling without craving.

86
00:05:55,423 --> 00:05:58,225
[SPEAKER_01]: But the only way to understand that is through abandoning the craving.

87
00:05:58,853 --> 00:06:02,116
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like finding out how to not crave on top of your craving.

88
00:06:02,896 --> 00:06:14,006
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only way to abandon, to understand how you can have feeling without craving, is to first, through craving, you discern a necessary basis of feeling, and then abandon craving by fully understanding that necessary basis.

89
00:06:14,586 --> 00:06:16,268
[SPEAKER_01]: That's exactly paticcaṁ pāḍhā, nothing else.

90
00:06:16,708 --> 00:06:23,754
[SPEAKER_01]: With this as a necessary basis, this is enduring, engaging me, making me act, making me feel, all of these things.

91
00:06:25,662 --> 00:06:30,124
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why there's so many different variations of paticca upādāna, the suttas.

92
00:06:30,324 --> 00:06:33,445
[SPEAKER_01]: Sixteen factors, twelve factors, eight factors sometimes.

93
00:06:33,505 --> 00:06:35,406
[SPEAKER_01]: Some omitted, some included, some added.

94
00:06:36,447 --> 00:06:38,187
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they are the principle.

95
00:06:38,588 --> 00:06:44,730
[SPEAKER_01]: Principle of discerning the necessary basis for another simultaneously enduring phenomenon.

96
00:06:44,770 --> 00:06:45,811
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not an explanation.

97
00:06:46,331 --> 00:06:52,834
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a... it's not a... it's not like a linear sort of set of visualizations you have to go through in order to practice it.

98
00:06:53,074 --> 00:06:53,494
[SPEAKER_01]: None of that.

99
00:06:54,715 --> 00:06:55,215
[SPEAKER_01]: It's discerning.

100
00:06:55,275 --> 00:06:56,356
[SPEAKER_01]: What is my present situation?

101
00:06:56,836 --> 00:06:57,997
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there a sense-desire in me?

102
00:06:58,757 --> 00:06:58,897
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

103
00:06:59,558 --> 00:07:00,979
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there absence of sense-desire in me?

104
00:07:01,339 --> 00:07:01,539
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

105
00:07:02,199 --> 00:07:03,800
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the present feeling on account of that?

106
00:07:05,521 --> 00:07:07,082
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there any desire towards that feeling?

107
00:07:07,622 --> 00:07:10,044
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now I have a peaceful feeling, neutral feeling.

108
00:07:10,524 --> 00:07:13,666
[SPEAKER_01]: Do I have desire to sort of want that feeling more?

109
00:07:15,107 --> 00:07:17,728
[SPEAKER_01]: In which case, well, it's not any more neutral, it's unpleasant now.

110
00:07:21,068 --> 00:07:28,312
[SPEAKER_00]: And in regard to the body, seeing the body and external objects?

111
00:07:28,332 --> 00:07:28,892
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

112
00:07:28,972 --> 00:07:37,136
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you even fathom, can you even think, imagine, in utter complete abstraction, an external object without the necessary basis of your body?

113
00:07:38,877 --> 00:07:39,458
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

114
00:07:41,599 --> 00:07:46,021
[SPEAKER_01]: So, seeing those two... Well, seeing basically the right order of things.

115
00:07:46,141 --> 00:07:50,063
[SPEAKER_01]: Seeing this within this, not this within that.

116
00:07:51,477 --> 00:07:59,301
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the difference in the... in Bhattacarya, like, when not seen, then, yeah, your feelings are a result of your craving, because that's how you assume them.

117
00:07:59,881 --> 00:08:15,288
[SPEAKER_01]: They're not, but you don't know that they're not, and because of that, the only reality you know is the reality that, yeah, you are craving and all this... That's why it's like a vicious circle, until you understand that your craving cannot make a difference to the nature of being subjected to the feeling.

118
00:08:15,952 --> 00:08:18,734
[SPEAKER_01]: And that your craving can never step outside of the feeling.

119
00:08:18,874 --> 00:08:23,737
[SPEAKER_01]: It requires feeling as a necessary container for it to endure, for it to manifest.

120
00:08:29,320 --> 00:08:38,126
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't want to be doing, reflecting upon what this is all about, like, on random things, because it's already subtle enough to be understood.

121
00:08:41,067 --> 00:08:42,708
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're going to just make it more abstract.

122
00:08:42,728 --> 00:08:45,130
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to find practical.

123
00:08:46,020 --> 00:08:46,521
[SPEAKER_01]: side of it.

124
00:08:46,641 --> 00:08:52,890
[SPEAKER_01]: Practical is feeling freedom from it, as in freedom from craving in regard to it, which is freedom from suffering.

125
00:08:54,072 --> 00:08:56,796
[SPEAKER_01]: Because one who sees the dhamma sees bhakti-sampāda.

126
00:08:56,836 --> 00:08:58,318
[SPEAKER_01]: One who sees bhakti-sampāda sees the dhamma.

127
00:09:01,303 --> 00:09:03,304
[SPEAKER_01]: So, literally, the entire dhamma is in that principle.

128
00:09:03,885 --> 00:09:06,327
[SPEAKER_01]: And why is that principle so important, so profound?

129
00:09:06,367 --> 00:09:22,839
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, if you see that your presently enduring desire, fully embedded in the attitude of your craving for this, craving for this to not be, craving for this to be, if you see all of that, that experience in its entirety,

130
00:09:23,577 --> 00:09:46,058
[SPEAKER_01]: As fully contained, fully determined, formed, the saṅkhara, by the presently enduring feeling that you've been subjected to, you haven't created it, you realize the utmost of your desires and cravings and wills, it's actually rooted in something else, not you, and thus cannot be yours, even if you engage with it.

131
00:09:48,752 --> 00:09:53,173
[SPEAKER_01]: But by not dwelling on that long enough, you do develop the notion of I am.

132
00:09:53,953 --> 00:09:58,854
[SPEAKER_01]: My own possessions, my own feelings, my own cravings, my own desires, and everything else is the result of that.

133
00:10:00,254 --> 00:10:01,074
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's the other way around.

134
00:10:01,694 --> 00:10:04,695
[SPEAKER_01]: So you want to start including it in a bigger context.

135
00:10:06,055 --> 00:10:12,856
[SPEAKER_01]: Context that, yeah, your mind might be feverish with the desire for this particular object.

136
00:10:12,916 --> 00:10:15,997
[SPEAKER_01]: Peripheral to that is the necessity of the attitude of craving.

137
00:10:16,097 --> 00:10:18,297
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you have desire without craving for what you desire?

138
00:10:18,661 --> 00:10:18,801
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

139
00:10:21,222 --> 00:10:24,604
[SPEAKER_01]: So you realize, okay, so whenever there is a desire, I know craving is there.

140
00:10:25,785 --> 00:10:27,426
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't need to see it written on the wall.

141
00:10:27,506 --> 00:10:28,987
[SPEAKER_01]: Craving... No, I know.

142
00:10:29,147 --> 00:10:30,528
[SPEAKER_01]: Desire is present means craving is here.

143
00:10:30,868 --> 00:10:33,009
[SPEAKER_01]: Desire is a symptom of the attitude of craving.

144
00:10:34,470 --> 00:10:38,312
[SPEAKER_01]: Once you become familiar with that, you take it a step further, but the same shit.

145
00:10:39,252 --> 00:10:39,833
[SPEAKER_01]: Same principle.

146
00:10:40,354 --> 00:10:40,975
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what we meant.

147
00:10:41,115 --> 00:10:43,219
[SPEAKER_01]: You learn how to attend properly.

148
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:49,950
[SPEAKER_01]: So, instead of attending at what craving is pushing you towards, you attend at the necessary basis for the push of the craving.

149
00:10:50,987 --> 00:10:59,051
[SPEAKER_01]: That yoniso manasikāra, you attend to the womb, to the back, to the origin of this attitude that I have, that I'm not denying, but I'm not engaging with it either.

150
00:10:59,631 --> 00:11:07,695
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where sensory strain comes first, then you get to understand, and then you just get to be drilling and drilling the same understanding through the same way of properly attending.

151
00:11:08,035 --> 00:11:09,196
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the noble method.

152
00:11:09,336 --> 00:11:10,736
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the noble method, exactly.

153
00:11:12,197 --> 00:11:13,678
[SPEAKER_01]: As concise as you can explain it.

154
00:11:17,079 --> 00:11:18,600
[SPEAKER_00]: There's another section here.

155
00:11:23,290 --> 00:11:28,115
[SPEAKER_00]: In regard to the body, it says, when there is external name and matter, there is body.

156
00:11:28,796 --> 00:11:31,559
[SPEAKER_00]: When there isn't external name and matter, there isn't body.

157
00:11:33,001 --> 00:11:35,484
[SPEAKER_00]: When external name and matter arises, the body arises.

158
00:11:35,624 --> 00:11:38,087
[SPEAKER_00]: When external name and matter ceases, the body ceases.

159
00:11:39,675 --> 00:11:47,296
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you were seeing that, that's your no... if you were attending that description, that's the noble method.

160
00:11:47,416 --> 00:11:57,618
[SPEAKER_01]: By seeing that necessity of presently arising experience as a whole, be it, like in this case, your body, is rooted in a phenomenon different than your point of view, different than you.

161
00:11:58,618 --> 00:12:01,059
[SPEAKER_01]: And when I say rooted, I mean simultaneously rooted.

162
00:12:01,079 --> 00:12:02,759
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the important... That's why it's so hard to see.

163
00:12:03,719 --> 00:12:06,740
[SPEAKER_01]: Because we... we can understand causation.

164
00:12:08,611 --> 00:12:11,873
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, this is the basis and this is the result.

165
00:12:13,213 --> 00:12:21,217
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's hard to understand, like, a simultaneously enduring presence of two things determining each other.

166
00:12:21,317 --> 00:12:23,018
[SPEAKER_01]: If you look at this one, it's determined by this one.

167
00:12:23,438 --> 00:12:25,019
[SPEAKER_01]: If you look at that one, it's determined by the other one.

168
00:12:25,839 --> 00:12:28,941
[SPEAKER_01]: So, determination is opposite from where you look.

169
00:12:28,981 --> 00:12:30,281
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the simile the Buddha gave.

170
00:12:30,301 --> 00:12:31,942
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like two sticks holding each other.

171
00:12:31,982 --> 00:12:33,583
[SPEAKER_01]: You remove one, the other one cannot remain.

172
00:12:34,215 --> 00:12:36,596
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not one stick comes first, then the other one follows.

173
00:12:36,716 --> 00:12:38,476
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the two sticks, determined to that extent.

174
00:12:38,496 --> 00:12:39,956
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not a cause and effect.

175
00:12:40,116 --> 00:12:41,197
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a cause and effect.

176
00:12:41,257 --> 00:12:41,777
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

177
00:12:42,397 --> 00:12:48,658
[SPEAKER_01]: It's whatever has presently arisen, within that, you also discern presently enduring basis for it.

178
00:12:51,439 --> 00:12:54,000
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can't have that basis without this thing being there.

179
00:12:54,020 --> 00:13:03,602
[SPEAKER_01]: How would you be discerning, discerning, oh, my craving has been determined, like, my craving to get rid of this has been determined by an unpleasant feeling that also endures.

180
00:13:04,488 --> 00:13:10,071
[SPEAKER_01]: How would you discern that, if you haven't discerned the craving to get rid of this first?

181
00:13:14,173 --> 00:13:22,678
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you get to arrive at the determination, the necessary basis, the necessary container, on account of the presently arising phenomena, that you became mindful of properly.

182
00:13:22,778 --> 00:13:28,261
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, you haven't given in to it by default, you haven't acted out of it, you became aware of its own endurance.

183
00:13:29,180 --> 00:13:31,782
[SPEAKER_01]: And you were not habitually trying to get rid of it.

184
00:13:32,682 --> 00:13:33,263
[SPEAKER_01]: I must deal with this.

185
00:13:33,303 --> 00:13:33,883
[SPEAKER_01]: I must deal with this.

186
00:13:33,903 --> 00:13:34,363
[SPEAKER_01]: I must deal with this.

187
00:13:34,383 --> 00:13:34,884
[SPEAKER_01]: Craving is bad.

188
00:13:34,904 --> 00:13:35,344
[SPEAKER_01]: Craving is bad.

189
00:13:35,384 --> 00:13:35,864
[SPEAKER_01]: Craving is bad.

190
00:13:36,345 --> 00:13:36,525
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

191
00:13:36,545 --> 00:13:37,205
[SPEAKER_01]: Craving is bad.

192
00:13:37,705 --> 00:13:40,887
[SPEAKER_01]: But you trying to get rid of craving cannot be rooted in anything other than craving.

193
00:13:41,468 --> 00:13:44,590
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, you trying to get rid of craving keeps perpetuating the craving.

194
00:13:48,192 --> 00:13:49,473
[SPEAKER_00]: But that you need craving.

195
00:13:50,233 --> 00:13:52,075
[SPEAKER_00]: Craving for freedom.

196
00:13:52,175 --> 00:13:53,075
[SPEAKER_01]: You need something.

197
00:13:53,095 --> 00:13:53,315
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

198
00:13:54,536 --> 00:13:55,757
[SPEAKER_01]: You need craving for freedom.

199
00:13:57,880 --> 00:14:00,702
[SPEAKER_01]: Like a... as a... as a... as a start-up for your practice.

200
00:14:01,202 --> 00:14:10,528
[SPEAKER_01]: It can then eventually get you closer to learning how to attend to it properly, so you can actually free yourself from craving without craving it.

201
00:14:11,368 --> 00:14:13,189
[SPEAKER_01]: And this is how you do it, as I was describing it.

202
00:14:14,210 --> 00:14:19,113
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you have to want to crave the freedom from suffering, which is another craving, and you'll suffer on account of it.

203
00:14:19,993 --> 00:14:21,194
[SPEAKER_01]: But because of that suffering,

204
00:14:22,960 --> 00:14:27,423
[SPEAKER_01]: On account of craving to be free from suffering, you will keep looking for the way out of suffering.

205
00:14:27,523 --> 00:14:32,207
[SPEAKER_01]: You will keep reading the suttas, you will keep practicing, you will keep reflecting, you keep... you will be keeping the precepts.

206
00:14:33,147 --> 00:14:36,169
[SPEAKER_01]: And all of that is actually bringing you closer to be free from suffering.

207
00:14:36,930 --> 00:14:38,471
[SPEAKER_01]: Bringing you closer to understand the craving.

208
00:14:38,691 --> 00:14:39,372
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha said that.

209
00:14:39,412 --> 00:14:41,373
[SPEAKER_01]: Craving is to be abandoned by the means of craving.

210
00:14:41,773 --> 00:14:44,315
[SPEAKER_01]: Conceit is to be abandoned by the means of conceit.

211
00:14:44,976 --> 00:14:49,139
[SPEAKER_01]: You hear, then, such and such became Sotāpanna, Rādhārāṇī and say, oh, if he can do it,

212
00:14:50,928 --> 00:14:52,170
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a conceited thought.

213
00:14:52,270 --> 00:14:55,834
[SPEAKER_01]: But because of that thought, you actually might get and start making effort.

214
00:14:57,576 --> 00:15:01,080
[SPEAKER_01]: Start taking it more seriously, keeping the precepts more strictly, all that.

215
00:15:01,420 --> 00:15:03,482
[SPEAKER_01]: And because of that, you will be free from conceit then.

216
00:15:04,143 --> 00:15:10,130
[SPEAKER_01]: See, it was your desire, conceited desire, that you wisely directed against itself, so to speak.

217
00:15:12,280 --> 00:15:14,941
[SPEAKER_01]: But, in order to do that, you need to know what you're supposed to do.

218
00:15:15,001 --> 00:15:16,061
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to know what the practice is.

219
00:15:16,101 --> 00:15:22,543
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't just be randomly denying, affirming, wanting, not wanting, and then hoping that the results will magically manifest.

220
00:15:23,663 --> 00:15:27,024
[SPEAKER_01]: Only through understanding of what the problem is, you get free yourself from the problem.

221
00:15:28,684 --> 00:15:29,664
[SPEAKER_01]: So, suffering is the problem.

222
00:15:29,684 --> 00:15:31,765
[SPEAKER_01]: The most obvious problem, because it's felt unpleasantly.

223
00:15:32,425 --> 00:15:33,585
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so now understand it.

224
00:15:33,965 --> 00:15:35,726
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just not feel it, but understand it.

225
00:15:38,286 --> 00:15:40,847
[SPEAKER_01]: Freedom from suffering doesn't mean you never feel unpleasantly.

226
00:15:42,208 --> 00:15:44,870
[SPEAKER_01]: It means you don't suffer on account of the unpleasant feeling.

227
00:15:46,632 --> 00:15:47,913
[SPEAKER_01]: So, and that's achieved.

228
00:15:47,933 --> 00:15:55,819
[SPEAKER_01]: Not by not feeling anything, as in no feeling manifested, but it's manifested through understanding whatever feeling is there.

229
00:15:57,560 --> 00:16:02,925
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you understand the characteristics of the feeling, as we just started this talk, you will be unable to crave it.

230
00:16:03,365 --> 00:16:05,427
[SPEAKER_01]: And if there is no craving, there's no feeling.

231
00:16:05,447 --> 00:16:06,207
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry, there's no suffering.

232
00:16:24,609 --> 00:16:24,869
[SPEAKER_00]: It was...

233
00:16:25,129 --> 00:16:26,150
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't find it.

234
00:16:27,650 --> 00:16:37,434
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you know, it's one who sees mindfulness of the body, sees the deathless.

235
00:16:41,095 --> 00:16:43,196
[SPEAKER_01]: The deathless.

236
00:16:43,336 --> 00:16:48,858
[SPEAKER_01]: Mindfulness of the body means understanding of the nature of the body.

237
00:16:48,879 --> 00:16:52,620
[SPEAKER_01]: When you say body, my body, whatever that designation refers to,

238
00:16:54,363 --> 00:16:55,144
[SPEAKER_01]: You have understood it.

239
00:16:56,025 --> 00:17:04,172
[SPEAKER_01]: Through that understanding, that is how you partake in deathless, the nibbāna, as in dispassion, absence, impossibility to crave.

240
00:17:09,337 --> 00:17:10,318
[SPEAKER_00]: See the deathless.

241
00:17:12,259 --> 00:17:13,881
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you can't like see the deathless.

242
00:17:14,501 --> 00:17:16,043
[SPEAKER_01]: You partake in it through understanding it.

243
00:17:17,509 --> 00:17:24,693
[SPEAKER_01]: But, as I said, all these things that you experience in your day-to-day life, your own craving, if you properly attend to it, will be pertaining to deathless.

244
00:17:24,833 --> 00:17:27,794
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, absence of this is what presence of nibbāna is.

245
00:17:28,655 --> 00:17:29,395
[SPEAKER_01]: Absence of craving.

246
00:17:30,656 --> 00:17:31,516
[SPEAKER_01]: Absence of craving.

247
00:17:32,136 --> 00:17:35,658
[SPEAKER_01]: So, to the extent that your craving is absent, to that extent, nibbāna is present.

248
00:17:38,560 --> 00:17:42,361
[SPEAKER_01]: If your craving had been fully uprooted, nibbāna has been fully developed.

249
00:17:44,703 --> 00:17:45,303
[SPEAKER_00]: It's automatic.

250
00:17:50,365 --> 00:17:52,147
[SPEAKER_01]: But only through understanding the nature of craving.

251
00:17:53,368 --> 00:18:00,615
[SPEAKER_01]: Not through following blind method, technique or something that we just be doing and then somehow your craving is magically diminishing.

252
00:18:02,136 --> 00:18:07,462
[SPEAKER_01]: Your craving diminishes when you understand what diminishing of craving is.

253
00:18:07,482 --> 00:18:12,847
[SPEAKER_00]: So if... so when you are free from craving...

254
00:18:17,803 --> 00:18:19,124
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, paticampāda remains.

255
00:18:19,825 --> 00:18:22,507
[SPEAKER_01]: The difference in paticampāda is not presence of the principle or not.

256
00:18:22,587 --> 00:18:23,428
[SPEAKER_01]: Principle is always there.

257
00:18:23,588 --> 00:18:24,449
[SPEAKER_01]: With this, this is.

258
00:18:24,549 --> 00:18:25,349
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how things work.

259
00:18:26,090 --> 00:18:31,955
[SPEAKER_01]: But the... With ignorance, paticampāda is referred to be as with the grain.

260
00:18:32,816 --> 00:18:34,237
[SPEAKER_01]: So, with feeling, craving is.

261
00:18:34,277 --> 00:18:35,458
[SPEAKER_01]: With craving, you have desire.

262
00:18:35,498 --> 00:18:40,062
[SPEAKER_01]: With desire, you have fighting, and protecting, and losing, and suffering, and all these things.

263
00:18:41,702 --> 00:18:49,611
[SPEAKER_01]: Without feeling, as in without feeling being appropriated as mine, with feeling that's fully understood in its nature that cannot be owned, there is no craving.

264
00:18:50,031 --> 00:18:51,312
[SPEAKER_01]: Without craving, there's no desire.

265
00:18:52,354 --> 00:18:55,777
[SPEAKER_01]: So, with this container present, this is present within it.

266
00:18:56,538 --> 00:18:59,001
[SPEAKER_01]: With the absence of that container, this is absent within it.

267
00:18:59,942 --> 00:19:02,845
[SPEAKER_01]: But the principle of that determination remains the same.

268
00:19:03,918 --> 00:19:17,246
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the principle, could you... could a person say... what would you say, if someone said that, that the principle is the... what is behind that principle?

269
00:19:18,127 --> 00:19:19,327
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the ultimate ground?

270
00:19:19,367 --> 00:19:20,688
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the ultimate...

271
00:19:21,288 --> 00:19:22,589
[SPEAKER_01]: The two sticks holding each other.

272
00:19:22,609 --> 00:19:22,769
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

273
00:19:23,290 --> 00:19:27,012
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there something beyond... See, the principle, you cannot make principle into one thing.

274
00:19:27,172 --> 00:19:28,193
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

275
00:19:29,503 --> 00:19:38,953
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you say, what is the ultimate behind this principle, means, what I described as a principle of two simultaneous things, you're regarding as one, and I'm trying to determine what's behind it.

276
00:19:39,273 --> 00:19:41,635
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever it is, that's what the principle is.

277
00:19:42,216 --> 00:19:45,579
[SPEAKER_01]: The relationship of these two things, simultaneously present.

278
00:19:47,241 --> 00:19:50,665
[SPEAKER_00]: Because even if you think there is this one ultimate ground,

279
00:19:52,009 --> 00:19:54,510
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you determine that... yeah, exactly.

280
00:19:54,831 --> 00:19:56,952
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you know the ultimate ground without thinking it?

281
00:19:57,012 --> 00:20:05,476
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, that thought is already secondary, basically, to whatever you're feeling, whatever you're craving, whatever your views are, whatever your attention is.

282
00:20:07,457 --> 00:20:10,979
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only... the way to discern the principle correctly,

283
00:20:11,712 --> 00:20:15,594
[SPEAKER_01]: Is to start including your way of attending to things in it.

284
00:20:16,534 --> 00:20:20,956
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, you choosing to attend this, is already within the principle.

285
00:20:21,516 --> 00:20:26,718
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't just leave your attention out and then discern the principle, because that becomes explanation.

286
00:20:27,518 --> 00:20:29,019
[SPEAKER_01]: That becomes psychologization.

287
00:20:29,939 --> 00:20:31,300
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it's not what it's about.

288
00:20:31,360 --> 00:20:36,662
[SPEAKER_00]: It is to further... as far as the person can see, like in terms of phenomenology.

289
00:20:38,078 --> 00:20:38,758
[SPEAKER_00]: That principle.

290
00:20:38,998 --> 00:20:41,199
[SPEAKER_01]: But there, you can't... Yeah, you can't step outside of it.

291
00:20:41,459 --> 00:20:41,659
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

292
00:20:41,859 --> 00:20:42,899
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't step outside of it.

293
00:20:43,479 --> 00:20:59,062
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why we keep talking about peripheral awareness and practicing mindfulness, and not like unmindful, unmindful, but recognize your situation, see whatever you're attending, but keep a corner of your eye on the background, as in of how you're feeling, what the current mood endures, because that is what's directing your attention.

294
00:20:59,682 --> 00:21:05,803
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if you're un... if you are unmindful, like if you... if you haven't recollected the right context,

295
00:21:06,981 --> 00:21:08,562
[SPEAKER_01]: That craving is bad, for example.

296
00:21:09,003 --> 00:21:10,384
[SPEAKER_01]: The craving results in suffering.

297
00:21:10,464 --> 00:21:19,010
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't have that context in your mind, enduring, when craving is present, you're going down the rabbit hole of your desire for whatever it wants.

298
00:21:20,851 --> 00:21:28,157
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your attention is fully sent and directed and thrown by that lack of understanding of the context of craving.

299
00:21:29,968 --> 00:21:36,818
[SPEAKER_01]: If you do maintain the context that craving, it should be unwanted, because it always means... presence of craving means presence of suffering.

300
00:21:36,898 --> 00:21:38,060
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, craving means suffering.

301
00:21:38,801 --> 00:21:40,323
[SPEAKER_01]: Desire is suffering in itself.

302
00:21:40,404 --> 00:21:42,386
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you give in to it, try to get rid of it.

303
00:21:43,392 --> 00:21:44,513
[SPEAKER_01]: You maintain that context.

304
00:21:44,893 --> 00:21:44,993
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah!

305
00:21:45,333 --> 00:21:46,994
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now you are aware.

306
00:21:47,054 --> 00:21:54,158
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you are attending to what present state of mind endures, such as state of sensual desire or state of ill-will.

307
00:21:54,878 --> 00:22:01,622
[SPEAKER_01]: But that attention is not going all the way, because you are mindful of the context that sort of is a necessary container for it.

308
00:22:03,257 --> 00:22:09,103
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're already, even unknowingly to that extent, certainly in the beginning, you're practicing the principle of Padisumpana rightly.

309
00:22:09,524 --> 00:22:12,707
[SPEAKER_01]: You're going against the grain, as the Buddha said.

310
00:22:13,428 --> 00:22:19,675
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, there is wanting, but you are now aware of the context that it's a necessary basis for your wanting.

311
00:22:21,279 --> 00:22:28,226
[SPEAKER_01]: And the only way you can arrive at it is to first restrain the actions that are rooted in you, the actions that come out as a result of your wants.

312
00:22:28,727 --> 00:22:35,154
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why sīla, virtue, sense restraint, is a necessary basis and must be developed beforehand.

313
00:22:35,694 --> 00:22:38,257
[SPEAKER_01]: Habit of, no, I will not engage with sensuality.

314
00:22:38,818 --> 00:22:43,382
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I will not engage in ill-will, in gossip, in malicious speech, in slander and all these things.

315
00:22:44,263 --> 00:22:53,609
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't know why, but you take it on trust that you won't do it, and because you haven't been doing it, you're training yourself to not automatically act out of present near isn't desire.

316
00:22:54,270 --> 00:22:59,233
[SPEAKER_01]: By not acting automatically, you immediately face this... force yourself to see the context of it.

317
00:23:00,374 --> 00:23:04,617
[SPEAKER_01]: By acting automatically, you immediately jump down the rabbit hole and you don't see any context.

318
00:23:04,717 --> 00:23:06,198
[SPEAKER_01]: It gets narrow and narrow and narrow.

319
00:23:06,638 --> 00:23:09,220
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly what sensuality is.

320
00:23:09,240 --> 00:23:09,720
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will.

321
00:23:09,780 --> 00:23:11,962
[SPEAKER_01]: It is complete loss of perspective.

322
00:23:19,180 --> 00:23:20,160
[SPEAKER_00]: Non-duality.

323
00:23:20,861 --> 00:23:22,361
[SPEAKER_00]: See, that's the ultimate ground.

324
00:23:23,041 --> 00:23:24,322
[SPEAKER_00]: Non-dual.

325
00:23:24,802 --> 00:23:26,903
[SPEAKER_00]: So, patisampara.

326
00:23:27,803 --> 00:23:28,863
[SPEAKER_00]: You said it's duality.

327
00:23:29,183 --> 00:23:29,484
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course.

328
00:23:30,124 --> 00:23:34,465
[SPEAKER_01]: The only way you can... How can you... Like, see, how can you be aware of non-duality?

329
00:23:36,106 --> 00:23:41,788
[SPEAKER_01]: Because your awareness is already different from that non-duality, which means there are two things, but you're just not ignoring one.

330
00:23:42,478 --> 00:23:51,580
[SPEAKER_01]: You're ignoring your point of view, you're ignoring your perspective, you're ignoring your own awareness of the thing that has or isn't, even if that's the ultimate reality of non-duality.

331
00:23:52,241 --> 00:23:53,881
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but you're aware of it, thus it's dual.

332
00:23:55,181 --> 00:24:04,044
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you were to see that whatever is the object of my attention, including non-duality, the ultimate nibbāna, means attention and object of it.

333
00:24:04,464 --> 00:24:08,185
[SPEAKER_01]: Can I attend it if the object is... if I haven't been subjected to the object?

334
00:24:08,345 --> 00:24:08,485
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

335
00:24:10,185 --> 00:24:11,806
[SPEAKER_01]: Would I know the object if I'm not attending it?

336
00:24:12,026 --> 00:24:12,166
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

337
00:24:12,674 --> 00:24:15,637
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, two sticks determine each other depending on where you look.

338
00:24:17,779 --> 00:24:23,024
[SPEAKER_01]: And there was never... it's never the point to get your attention to attend to the origin or something of itself.

339
00:24:23,144 --> 00:24:24,025
[SPEAKER_01]: That's inconceivable.

340
00:24:24,925 --> 00:24:28,409
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to understand the background of what you're attending.

341
00:24:29,189 --> 00:24:32,553
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what it... where the determination of your attention is.

342
00:24:33,834 --> 00:24:35,055
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're attending non-duality.

343
00:24:35,095 --> 00:24:36,076
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the background of it?

344
00:24:37,376 --> 00:24:52,108
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's certain desire, isn't it, of... towards pleasure, towards the ideal of ultimate peace, of non-duality, merging with the greater universe, reality, whatever you want to call it.

345
00:24:53,076 --> 00:24:53,897
[SPEAKER_01]: It's motivated by that.

346
00:24:53,937 --> 00:24:54,678
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's present there.

347
00:24:54,758 --> 00:25:05,371
[SPEAKER_01]: Those are your intentions that make you think about non-duality, that you're clearly fully ignoring by over-committing to the reality of non-duality, which is, again, how can you know it unless you can think it?

348
00:25:05,752 --> 00:25:07,214
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you think it without attending it?

349
00:25:07,694 --> 00:25:09,917
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're attending something, your attention is not it.

350
00:25:11,902 --> 00:25:20,807
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the true non-duality then, even from that ideal of non-duality, would be complete unawareness of anything.

351
00:25:21,327 --> 00:25:22,908
[SPEAKER_01]: It would be a complete non-existence.

352
00:25:24,049 --> 00:25:26,971
[SPEAKER_01]: Because as soon as you're aware of something, to that extent, there is a difference.

353
00:25:29,352 --> 00:25:30,893
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you say, oh, it's non-existent.

354
00:25:30,913 --> 00:25:32,174
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you think non-existent?

355
00:25:32,274 --> 00:25:33,434
[SPEAKER_01]: Because your thought is existing.

356
00:25:34,175 --> 00:25:36,476
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're thinking non-existent by existing it?

357
00:25:36,936 --> 00:25:37,997
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a contradiction in terms.

358
00:25:38,568 --> 00:25:46,855
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, here you are, maintaining the attitude that, when you think non-existence, when you think non-duality, when you think the ultimate reality, that is what ultimate reality is.

359
00:25:47,375 --> 00:25:48,576
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, it's only your thought.

360
00:25:49,617 --> 00:25:50,918
[SPEAKER_00]: Cannot be anything different than that.

361
00:25:52,519 --> 00:26:01,827
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why we never... the issue was never finding the reality hidden behind, or running into some new astral... astrally projected realm of non-suffering.

362
00:26:02,468 --> 00:26:02,588
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

363
00:26:02,868 --> 00:26:07,552
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just, literally, stop misconceiving things as yours, through wrongly attending to them.

364
00:26:08,467 --> 00:26:09,087
[SPEAKER_01]: What things?

365
00:26:09,668 --> 00:26:11,849
[SPEAKER_01]: Your feelings, your intentions, your perceptions.

366
00:26:12,329 --> 00:26:12,649
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

367
00:26:12,949 --> 00:26:13,409
[SPEAKER_01]: Your mind.

368
00:26:17,211 --> 00:26:19,032
[SPEAKER_01]: That's... that is the ultimate.

369
00:26:20,453 --> 00:26:28,257
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's... that sutta where the Buddha says... he looks for who... who can he respect in the world, higher than him?

370
00:26:28,497 --> 00:26:29,057
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah.

371
00:26:29,237 --> 00:26:29,557
[SPEAKER_00]: Nobody.

372
00:26:30,138 --> 00:26:30,538
[SPEAKER_00]: Nobody.

373
00:26:30,598 --> 00:26:32,219
[SPEAKER_00]: But then he says, why don't I...

374
00:26:32,819 --> 00:26:33,459
[SPEAKER_00]: Respect the Dhamma.

375
00:26:33,599 --> 00:26:34,440
[SPEAKER_00]: Respect the Dhamma.

376
00:26:34,800 --> 00:26:36,841
[SPEAKER_00]: The principle that he discerned made him the Buddha.

377
00:26:38,170 --> 00:26:41,051
[SPEAKER_00]: And so he, he dwelt respecting that.

378
00:26:41,291 --> 00:26:41,532
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

379
00:26:41,552 --> 00:26:42,032
[SPEAKER_01]: Seeing that.

380
00:26:42,612 --> 00:26:56,979
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because if that principle, if things were not actually like this, as in, if you were not subjected to feeling, first, as a necessary enduring container for your craving in regard to it, for your actions, for whatever else you have on top of it, you wouldn't be able to free yourself from it.

381
00:26:57,519 --> 00:26:58,860
[SPEAKER_01]: The feeling would truly be yours.

382
00:26:59,160 --> 00:27:02,181
[SPEAKER_01]: You would be its creator, its owner, its manifester.

383
00:27:02,962 --> 00:27:03,622
[SPEAKER_01]: But you're not.

384
00:27:05,383 --> 00:27:07,584
[SPEAKER_01]: You ignore the fact that you're not and that's what you think you are.

385
00:27:08,024 --> 00:27:12,965
[SPEAKER_01]: You stop ignoring it, you will stop thinking you are the master of it.

386
00:27:15,206 --> 00:27:19,067
[SPEAKER_01]: But as I said, you have to reverse the order.

387
00:27:21,207 --> 00:27:23,608
[SPEAKER_01]: So now, the only feeling you know is the feeling through your craving.

388
00:27:26,449 --> 00:27:31,030
[SPEAKER_01]: So when I first descended this craving there, as in leaning towards, leaning against,

389
00:27:32,450 --> 00:27:33,411
[SPEAKER_01]: Attitude of yes.

390
00:27:33,571 --> 00:27:34,351
[SPEAKER_01]: Attitude of no.

391
00:27:35,332 --> 00:27:36,332
[SPEAKER_01]: And, oh, I must get rid of it.

392
00:27:36,352 --> 00:27:36,492
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

393
00:27:36,752 --> 00:27:38,894
[SPEAKER_01]: I must discern the feeling within this attitude.

394
00:27:39,394 --> 00:27:42,736
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, that feeling will be seen as actually a necessary basis for this attitude.

395
00:27:44,577 --> 00:27:46,398
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you surmount the attitude of craving.

396
00:27:47,418 --> 00:27:48,339
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you surmount suffering.

397
00:27:49,179 --> 00:27:51,841
[SPEAKER_01]: And if that were not possible to do, you wouldn't have done it.

398
00:27:52,201 --> 00:27:52,941
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what we said.

399
00:27:53,282 --> 00:27:56,463
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why don't I then respect the fact that it was possible to do it?

400
00:27:57,624 --> 00:27:58,905
[SPEAKER_01]: As in the universal principle.

